%S A402160 0,0,5,194,6664,235768,8911844,355163980,14759702749,635429988362,
%T A402160 28205170243276,1285370158532292
%N A402160 Number of sets of three distinct triangulations of a convex n-gon such that the graph formed by the sides of the polygon and the diagonals of the three triangulations is not 4-colorable.
%C A402160 A triangulation of a convex polygon with vertices 0, 1, ..., n-1 (in cyclic order) is a set of n-3 diagonals no two of which cross; it divides the polygon into n-2 triangles. Triangulations that differ by a rotation or a reflection are counted as different, so there are A000108(n-2) of them. The graph of three triangulations has the n vertices of the polygon, its n sides and every diagonal that belongs to at least one of the three; points where diagonals cross are not vertices. A common coloring of the three triangulations is a coloring of the vertices in which the two ends of every edge of this graph get different colors, and the graph is 4-colorable if there is a common coloring with at most four colors.
%C A402160 Two triangulations always have a common coloring with four colors: draw the diagonals of one inside the polygon and those of the other outside, as arcs that do not cross; the result is a planar graph, and the four color theorem (see the Robertson et al. link) applies. Conversely, the four color theorem follows from the statement that every two triangulations of every polygon have a common coloring with four colors (see the links to Bowlin and Brin, to Kauffman, and to Cooper, Rowland and Zeilberger). So three is the smallest number of triangulations that can fail to have a common coloring with four colors. For example, a(6) = 194 means that 194 of the 364 sets of three triangulations of the hexagon fail, and a(14) is about 85.7% of the number of all sets for n = 14.
%C A402160 The proportion a(n)/binomial(A000108(n-2), 3) of failing sets (defined for n >= 5) tends to 1 as n tends to infinity, and the proportion of the other sets tends to zero exponentially fast. The reason is that, with probability tending to 1, some five consecutive vertices are cut off by a diagonal that belongs to all three triangulations, and inside this pentagon the three triangulations form one of the sets of the Example section that need five colors.
%C A402160 How many colors can a common coloring of three triangulations need? Six for the triangulations {02, 03, 35}, {04, 13, 14}, {15, 24, 25} of the hexagon (ij denotes the diagonal joining i and j): together they use all nine diagonals, so with the six sides every two vertices are joined. Eight colors always suffice (Bernhart and Kainen), and seven suffice for n <= 17; both facts follow from the bound 4*m-9 for the number of edges between any m >= 3 of the vertices. For n <= 12, an exhaustive search finds no set of three triangulations that needs more than six colors.
%C A402160 Whether six colors always suffice is an open problem. Let q be the largest number of colors needed by any set of three triangulations of any polygon. Then q is also the largest chromatic number of a graph of book thickness at most 3 (a graph whose vertices can be put on a line and whose edges, drawn as arcs above the line, can be split into three classes so that no two edges of the same class cross), and only 6 <= q <= 8 is known (Dujmović and Wood, Open Problem 2; Pupyrev). Although the value of q is not known, three triangulations of the n-gon chosen independently and uniformly at random need exactly q colors with probability tending to 1 as n tends to infinity.
%C A402160 Rooting a triangulation at the side joining 0 and n-1 turns it into a full binary tree with n-1 leaves (every vertex has no child or a left and a right child). Triangulations have a common coloring with four colors if and only if their trees have a common parse word in the sense of Cooper, Rowland and Zeilberger. A265279(m) is the number of unordered pairs of distinct such trees with m leaves that have exactly one common parse word, up to a permutation of the three letters used in the words.
%C A402160 All statements in the Comments and Formula sections, except the computed values and the open problem, are proved in the notes file in the Links section, which also gives sources and further data.
%H A402160 Frank Bernhart and Paul C. Kainen, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0095-8956(79)90021-2">The book thickness of a graph</a>, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 27 (1979), 320-331.
%H A402160 Garry Bowlin and Matthew G. Brin, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.3984">Coloring planar graphs via colored paths in the associahedra</a>, Int. J. Algebra Comput. 23 (2013), 1337-1418.
%H A402160 Bobbe Cooper, Eric Rowland and Doron Zeilberger, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.1324">Toward a language theoretic proof of the four color theorem</a>, Adv. Appl. Math. 48 (2012), 414-431.
%H A402160 Vida Dujmović and David R. Wood, <a href="https://doi.org/10.46298/dmtcs.317">On Linear Layouts of Graphs</a>, Discrete Math. Theor. Comput. Sci. 6 (2004), 339-358.
%H A402160 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402160/a402160.txt">Notes, proofs and sources</a>.
%H A402160 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402160/a402160_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%H A402160 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402160/a402160.png">Three triangulations of a hexagon with no common coloring in four colors</a>.
%H A402160 Louis H. Kauffman, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0095-8956(90)90114-F">Map coloring and the vector cross product</a>, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 48 (1990), 145-154.
%H A402160 Sergey Pupyrev, <a href="https://spupyrev.github.io/layoutproblems.html#chromatic-number-of-stack-graphs">Open Problems in Linear Graph Layouts</a>, online problem list, 2026.
%H A402160 Neil Robertson, Daniel P. Sanders, Paul D. Seymour and Robin Thomas, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1006/jctb.1997.1750">The Four-Colour Theorem</a>, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 70 (1997), 2-44.
%F A402160 a(n) = (A000108(n-2)^3 - b(n))/6, where b(n) is the number of ordered triples (T1, T2, T3) of triangulations of the n-gon, repetitions allowed, that have a common coloring with four colors. (A triple with a repetition always has one, and each set of three distinct triangulations gives six ordered triples.)
%F A402160 b(n)/A000108(n-2)^3 <= 1/A000108(n-2)^3 + (1 - 4^(-9))^floor((n-2)/5); hence a(n) ~ A000108(n-2)^3/6.
%F A402160 b(i+j-2) >= b(i)*b(j) for i, j >= 3; hence beta = lim_{n->infinity} b(n)^(1/n) exists. Moreover 28.8219 < beta < 63.9982 (for comparison, A000108(n-2)^(3/n) tends to 64); these bounds are far from determining beta.
%F A402160 a(n+1) >= a(n).
%F A402160 a(i+j-2) >= a(i)*A000108(j-2)^3 + a(j)*A000108(i-2)^3 - 6*a(i)*a(j) for i, j >= 3.
%F A402160 a(n) == 0 (mod n/gcd(n, 18)).
%e A402160 For n = 3 and n = 4 there are fewer than three triangulations, so a(3) = a(4) = 0.
%e A402160 For n = 5 the vertices are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, and ij denotes the diagonal joining i and j. There are five triangulations, each made of the two diagonals at one vertex: T0 = {02, 03}, T1 = {13, 14}, T2 = {02, 24}, T3 = {03, 13}, T4 = {14, 24}.
%e A402160 The set {T0, T1, T2} uses all five diagonals 02, 03, 13, 14, 24. With the five sides, every two vertices are then joined, so five colors are needed. In the same way {T1, T2, T3}, {T2, T3, T4}, {T3, T4, T0} and {T4, T0, T1} use all five diagonals and need five colors.
%e A402160 The other five sets {T0, T1, T3}, {T0, T2, T3}, {T0, T2, T4}, {T1, T2, T4}, {T1, T3, T4} miss the diagonal 24, 14, 13, 03, 02, respectively. The two ends of the missing diagonal can get the same color, and then four colors suffice. Hence a(5) = 5.
%o A402160 (Python)
%o A402160 from itertools import combinations
%o A402160 def crosses(e,f):
%o A402160     a,b=e;c,d=f
%o A402160     return a<c<b<d or c<a<d<b
%o A402160 def four_colorable(n,edges):
%o A402160     adj=[[u for u,v in edges if v==i] for i in range(n)]
%o A402160     colors=[]
%o A402160     def visit(used):
%o A402160         v=len(colors)
%o A402160         if v==n:return True
%o A402160         forbidden={colors[u] for u in adj[v]}
%o A402160         for c in range(min(4,used+1)):
%o A402160             if c not in forbidden:
%o A402160                 colors.append(c)
%o A402160                 if visit(max(used,c+1)):return True
%o A402160                 colors.pop()
%o A402160         return False
%o A402160     return visit(0)
%o A402160 for n in range(3,9):
%o A402160     sides={(i,i+1) for i in range(n-1)}|{(0,n-1)}
%o A402160     diagonals=set(combinations(range(n),2))-sides
%o A402160     triangulations=[set(t) for t in combinations(sorted(diagonals),n-3) if not any(crosses(e,f) for e,f in combinations(t,2))]
%o A402160     print(sum(not four_colorable(n,sides|a|b|c) for a,b,c in combinations(triangulations,3)))
